<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bartle Frere</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1815">1815</a>–<a href="/years/1884">1884</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Sir Henry Bartle Frere, Henry Bartle Frere, Sir Bartle Frere</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sir Bartle Frere was a British colonial administrator. After a career in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, where he was Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a>, he led a mission to Zanzibar in 1873 that pressed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> Barghash to sign a treaty ending the seaborne <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a>. As High Commissioner for Southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> from 1877 he provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-zulu-war">Anglo-Zulu War</a> of 1879 and was recalled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bartle-frere.jpg" alt="Bartle Frere" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George Reid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sir_(Henry)_Bartle_Frere,_1st_Bt_by_Sir_George_Reid.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frere was born on 29 March 1815 at Clydach House in Clydach, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monmouthshire">Monmouthshire</a>, the son of Edward Frere, manager of Clydach Ironworks, and Mary Ann Green; John Hookham Frere was his uncle. Educated at the East India Company College, he joined the Bombay Presidency civil service as a writer in 1834. In 1842 he became private secretary to Sir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-arthur">George Arthur</a>, Governor of Bombay, whose daughter Catherine Arthur he married on 10 October 1844.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>As political resident at the court of Raja Shahaji of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/satara">Satara</a>, he administered the province after the raja's death in 1848, before and after its annexation in 1849. Made chief commissioner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sindh">Sindh</a> in 1850, he reformed its postal system on the British model in 1851 and decreed that Sindhi replace Persian in government. In 1857, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-rebellion-of-1857">Indian Mutiny</a>, he sent detachments to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/multan">Multan</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, for which he received the thanks of Parliament and the KCB. As Governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mumbai">Bombay</a> from 1862 he founded the Deccan College at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pune">Pune</a> and had the ramparts of the old Bombay Fort pulled down so that the city could grow.</p><p>In 1877 Lord Carnarvon, the Secretary of State for the Colonies, made him High Commissioner for Southern Africa and governor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-colony">Cape Colony</a> to impose a confederation that the region's peoples and governments opposed. When a conflict broke out on the Cape frontier in 1877, Frere declared war on Gcalekaland, and in 1878, with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a>'s authority, he dismissed the Cape's elected government of John Charles Molteno and asked John Gordon Sprigg to form a ministry.</p><p>To make confederation succeed he sought the annexation of Zululand, timing his letters around the slow mail to circumvent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>'s opposition, and in December 1878 sent King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cetshwayo">Cetshwayo</a> an ultimatum he could not meet. After the disaster at Isandlwana he was censured but kept in office. His order disarming people of African descent led to the Basotho Gun War of 1880. Gladstone's government recalled him on 1 August 1880.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frere was preparing a fuller defence of his conduct when he died at Wimbledon on 29 May 1884, and he was buried in St Paul's Cathedral. Frere Hall in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karachi">Karachi</a> was built in his honour, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-of-wales">Prince of Wales</a> unveiled his statue on the Thames embankment in 1888, and Mount Bartle Frere in Queensland is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Henry Bartle Frere” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under pressure from the mission of <b>Sir Bartle Frere</b> and the threat of a British naval blockade, Barghash signed the treaty of 1873 that closed the Zanzibar slave market and banned the shipping of slaves from the mainland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sultanate-of-zanzibar">Sultanate of Zanzibar · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The high commissioner <b>Sir Henry Bartle Frere</b>, seeking a confederation of southern African states, regarded the Zulu army as a threat and in December 1878 issued an ultimatum demanding its disbandment.</blockquote><a href="/empire/zulu-kingdom">Zulu Kingdom · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Clydach House, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in Wimbledon, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=51.82,-3.11&amp;year=1859" aria-label="Clydach House on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4480 372 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Bartle Frere"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4930 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4930 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=51.82,-3.11&amp;year=1859">What was here in 1859? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
